\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 January 16, 1797 times of London - John Hetherington, haberdasher of the Strand was arraigned on a charge of breach of the peace and inciting to riot and required to post bond in the amount of 500 pounds for the following offesnse:}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It was in evidence that MMr. Hetherington\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85 appeared on the public highway wearing upon his head what he called a silk hat b(which was offered into evidence), a tall structure having a shining lustre, and calculated to frighten timitd people. As a matter of fact, the officers of the crown stated that several women fainted at the unusual sight, while children screamed, dogs yelped and a [young man] was thrown down by the crowd which had collected, and had his right arm broken. For these reasons the defnedant was seized by the guards and taken before the Lord Mayor.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The canons of menswear dictate that a black-tie ensemble consists of a black or midnight-blue jacket with a peak lapel or shawl collar faced in satin or grosgarain, and pants with the outside seam covered by a silk or grosgrain stripe. If the jacket is double breasted it must have a peak lapel. If it s single breasted, it can have a peak lapel or shawl collar, but never a notched lapel, which is characteristic of a more quotidian business suit. A cummerbund, worn so that its pleats face up must cover the waist, unless the jacket is double breated, inwhich case the cummerbund must not be worn. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85She pointed out that even oppositional subcultures that pride themselves on breaking all of the rules still \u8220\'93follow a code\u8230\'85the patches you put ona denim jacket or the pins, the band t-shirt you\u8217\'92re wearing is still code to other people to identify with\u8230\'85 I\u8217\'92m part of this tribe\u8230\'85In other words, those rule breakers replace the old rules with new rules\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 but this is missing the point. Everything is boundary negotiation. Its not so much replacing old rules with new rules, thus pushing their rebellion into irony, its that everyone operates within a tribe, and they feel excluded from one tribe, they create their own.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 From the late middle ages until the age of enlightenment, both law and custom required that clothing announce the social class, caste, occupation, religion, and, of course, gender of the wearer. These dress codes made clothing into status symbols, establishing a sartorial language that remains with us to this day.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In 1229 King louis VII of France ijmposed limits on the attire of the nobility in an effort to bring feudal lords under centralized control, and in 1279 King Phillippe le Hardi III restricted the luxuriousness of attire on a sliding scale according to the amount of land owned.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Crowns were of particular concern. In late-thirteenth century France, Philippe le Bel IV restricted the wearing of crowns to the upper echelons of sciety; his wife, Jeanne of Navarre, had on at least one occasion, remarked caustically on the prevalence of opulent attire; \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I believed myself to be the only queen and her I am seen with hundreds!\u8221\'94 \u8230\'85in 1439, an anonymous critic in Brescia complained that \u8220\'93builders, blacksmiths, pork-butchers, shoemakers, and weavers dressed their wives in crimson velvet, in silk, in damask, and finest scarlet; their sleeves, resembling widest banners, were lined with satin\u8230\'85.fitting only for kings on their heads pearls and the richest crowns glittered, crammed with gems\u8230\'85\u8221\'94}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In 1510 the first Parliament of Henry VIII passed \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93an act agaynst Wearing of Costly Apparrell.\u8221\'94 It was misleadingly named as it did not actually outlaw expensive attire; instead it restricted clothing of prestigious colors, refined quality, and exotic place of manufacture to people of high status. For instance, the act forbade men under degree of a lord to wear, \u8220\'93any cloth of gold or silver, sables or woollen cloth made of outside of England,Wales, Ireland, or Calais.\u8221\'94\u8230\'85}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Those who aided and abetted vestimentary villains faced legal sanctions as well. According to the terms of a proclamation of 1561, tailors and hosiers were forbidden to provide garments to those unauthorized to wear them and were required to post a bond of 40 pounds to guarantee compliance; in addition, their premises were to be searched once every eight days for contraband garments. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 reminded of the christian persecution fetish; no elites made laws outlawing EVERYTHING.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Because of its central focus on individual psychology rather tna heroic deeds, the novell is a democratic medium\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  a chronicle of the common person. While only monarchs, warriors, and sages play a role in the epic theater of geopolitics, everyone has a rich psychological life entangled in teh events of everyday life. The novel offers the wage laborer and middle manager the same attention and dignity it gives to the wealthy and powerful.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 1427, Firar Bernardino of Siena:}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 How do you know where to borrow money? By the sign on the awning. How do you know where wine is sold? By the sign. How do we find an inn? By its sign. You go to the taverner for wine because you see his sign. You say to him, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93give me some wine\u8230\'85Now, what of a woman who puts on clothes or decorates her head with vanities that are the sign of a whore? You\u8217\'92d ask for her\u8230\'85  you know what I mean, as you demand of a whore, if you like as demand wine from a taverner.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 French nuns adopted the traditional costume of the widow\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  a modest black dress adn veil\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  so that they could travel freely: while respectable single or married women required a male chaperone, law and custom allowed widows the liberty to travel alone. And in Catholic theology, nuns had the most inimpeachable  chaperone: as brides of Christ, their husband accompanied them everywhere.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Deuteronomy 22:5 - A woman shall not wear anything that pertains to a man, nor shall a man put on women\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s garment, for all who do are an abomination to the Lord your God, - this was the legal \u8216\'91heresy\u8217\'92 that got Joan of Arc burned at the stake.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The boor covers himself, the rich man or the fool adorns himself, and the elegant man gets dresed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 -Honore de balzac}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Chenoune notes that, in eithteenth-century England, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93ostentatiously expensive dress was associated with French fashion and Francophilia\u8230\'85 particularly following the Puritan revolutiona dn Cromwell\u8217\'92s Commonwealth that had condemned the British royalty to a forced twenty-year sojourn at the court of France.\u8221\'94 Indeed, when the English monarchy was restored in  1660, Charles II\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 having learned form his father\u8217\'92s tragic example\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 established a new, understated mode of dress: a vest and frock coat ensemble that became the prototype for the three-piece suti. Accordinga to historian David Kuchta, \u8220\'93In introducing the three-piece suit, Charles II attempted to appropriate an iconoclastic oppositional ideology first developed as a critique of hte monarchy and use it to redefine court culture\u8230\'85 cultural authority would be expressed by elite opposition to luxury, not by making conspicuous consumption the expclusive prerogative of the court.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 When Geolpolitical conflict between France and ENgland dampened French Anglophilia, French liberals looked to England\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s rebellious colonies: understated attire and a wigless head was dubbed fashion a la Franklin after Benjamin Franklin.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 highlander dress didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t really happen \u8220\'93kilt\u8221\'94 until 18th century englishman invented it, then coopted by elite highlanders later in the century - see pg. 89}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Proposals for national civilian uniforms went nowhere. But ironically, in the subsequent decades an international civilian uniform slowly developed, not through legislation or decree but through custom and practice. As Moser would have hoped, it became a sartorial sign of civic virtue worn by heads of states and low-level bereaucrats, titans of industry and wage-earning clerks, the cultural elite and the petite bourgeoisie, and it allowed for infinite variations to signify social rank. Within the span of roughly half a century, men from munich to manhattan adopted, en masse, this modest, matching trouser and jacket ensemble. The busniess suit became the livery and regalia of Western society, and, as the influence of the West spread, it would become the global symbol of medernity, industry, and Enlightenment.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Wigs were powdered with flour.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Jean Baptiste Thiers, cure of Champrond and Vibraque, decried the widespread wearing of wigs by the clergy, compaining that, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93so many ecclesiastics today wear a wig that there is every reason to believe that they are persuaded\u8230\'85 that this strange ornament is not wholly forbidden to them, and that it is not inherently unfitting to the seemliness of their profession.\u8221\'94 He went on to warn against the \u8220\'93seduction of appearances,\u8221\'94 insisting that wigs offended God by substituting human artifice for the blessing of nature; wasted time and resources in contradiction of the scriptural imperative for thrift; and betrayed the wearer\u8217\'92s vanity. This left the bewigged cleric unable, without inviting the charge of hypocrisy, to chastise the faithful for their luxurious clothing and other indulgences. According Kwass, the gentleman-economist Marquis de Mirabeau complained that wig-wearing members of the lower classes had put on airs: \u8220\'93Everyone is a Monsier\u8230\'85a man wearing black silk clothes and a well-powdered wig, and as I fell over myself offering him compliments, he introduced himself as the oldest son of my blacksmith\u8230\'85\u8221\'94}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 New social and political ideals of rationality, industriousness, and efficiency were displacing hte older values of spectacle and display and, accordingly, new subtle and unwritten dress codes began to privelege understatement over the conspicuous luxury that had been encoded in the laws of hte past. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 ..But of course, status hierarchy survived in a new form and so did the dress codes that made it visible. Hence the eighteenth and ninetheeth centuries saw the birth of a new suptuary code taht inverted the ancient values of the older ones: if the law could not deny luxury to upstarts and the nouveaux riches, then luxury alone would no longer be high status. From then on\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  and up to the present moment\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  a revers snobbery declared that too much opulence was a sign of poor taste and low breeding.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Beau Brummell \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The man whose name is synonymous with masculine fashion consciousness, Beau brummell, was famous for his understatement. His genius was to transform simplicity into an exacting perfection, requiring meticulous attention to costly details. George Bryan Burmmell was a commoner who made friends with the Prince of Wales while at Eton in the late eighteenth century and thereafter became a figure in London high society. Much about Brummell is shrouded in mystery and distorted in legend. It is said that after the prince granted him a commission in the elite 10th Regiment of the Light Dragoons\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  or \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Royal Hussars,\u8221\'94 as they were often called\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  Brummell insisted on altering the uniform to conform to his own standard of elegance. He was rumored to have hired to different glovers to produce a single pair of gloves: one to sew the fingers and another to construct the body. He was perhaps most well-known for the care with which he tied his cravat: he reportedly spent hours in front of a mirror; his floor littered with discarded scarves, which, having been creased in failed attempts at perfection, were unfit to be worn. Friends and admirers said that he took five hours to dress each day and he claimed to polish his boots with champagne. When asked the yearly cost to maintain the wardrobe of a well-dressed gentleman, Brummell answered that \u8220\'93with tolerable economy it might be done with 800 pounds\u8221\'94- the equivalent of 160,000 U.S. dollars today; at the time, the family of skilled craftsman lived on about 60 pounds a year.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Despite his legendary extravagence, Brummell\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s great distinction was the simplicity of his attire. In the aristocratic salons he frequented, most men still favored brocade, jewels, and other opulent adornments. Brummell, by contrast, wore a simple frock coat during the day and and a blue suit and white vest in the evening\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  an ensemble that \u8220\'93hardly varied, was not henached by jewelry or perfume, nor even discreetly highlighted by a special or distinctive detail.\u8221\'94 Brummell was, according to one of his contemporaries, \u8220\'93the most sober, most strict, and least extravagantly attired person of his acquaintance. Such unflagging moderation\u8230\'85made it impossible to imitate him, since there was nothing to copy.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Being well dreseed required both alot of clothing and a lot of knowledge: one needed a different ensemble for each of ahost of occasions and one needed to know which outfit went with a given event. Clothing was expensive and knowledge was closely guarded\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  usually conveyed by example or by word of mouth to those in the right social societies where bound books were luxury items and literacy still far from universal. The right clothing worn on the correct occasion was an unambiguous badge of hihg social position, while even small deviations from the rules signaled that either one\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s budget or one\u8217\'92s savoirfaire was inadequate. The greater the number and magnitude of the errors, the lower one\u8217\'92s rank in society would appear to be.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85the showy, overcompensating parvenu revealed his ignorance, insecurity, and ineptness; by contrast, the elegant person demonstrated her knowledge by wearing the right thing at the right time.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 holes in the brogue are for letting the water out because the shoues were worn walking in muddy streets and bogs.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 modern religions are like these shoes.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 As Anne Hollander observes, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Male clothing, and by extension male regard for personal appearcne altogether, continued to receive\u8230\'85 the respect given to all serious male enterprise\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 the terms of a new detente were established. Fashion was given over to the aesthetically intuitive but stylistically uninventive women who made and decorated sumptuous gowns and to the graceful but ornamental women who wore them. Tailoring was to remain the domain of skillfule male artisans who made functional and streamlined clouthing for the serious men of the world. From tne on, masculine and feminine fashions began to diverge, not only in their details, bu tin their fundamental symbolic vocabulary. The first, indispensable step in this divergence was to insist that masculine fashions wer not fashions at all.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The Great Masculine Renunciation was less an abandonment of luxury than it was an application of technological and aesthetic refinements that were transforming all of the visiual arts. THe simplification of the male wardrobe was part of a much broader shift away from ornamentation and formal literalism and toward a streamlined aesthetic that would mature into high modernism ath the begninning of the twentieth century\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85 It has been designed to }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 look}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  practical. By way of comparison, consider that the famous designs of modern architecture were often more impractical and more costly than the baroque and neoclassical structures they superseded: flat roofs }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 looked}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  streamlined abut were prone to leaks as a aresult of ponding water; the absence of ornate modling looked economical but actually required much more precies construction to elimante gaps and unevenness that modling sim ply covered; the seemingly unadorned structural columns of Mies Vander Rohe\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s iconic Seagram Buildinga re in fact needlessly clad in expensive and structurally superfluous bronze\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 sim ilarly, men\u8217\'92s tailoring can be as elaborate as many formal gowns\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  the difference is that while a gown exhibits its adornment on the outside of a draped garment, most of the intricate work in a suit is hidden in the seamwork, canvassing, and padding that gvie the ensemble its seemingly natural shape. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Macaroni hat - yankee doodle dandy- put a feather in his cap and called it macaroni.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The ninetheenth century dandy was typically an aristocrat, by predispositino an enemy of democracy and its leveling influences. Dandies were, of necessity, rich, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93with fortunes ample enough to pay without thinking of all their extravagances.\u8221\'94 But the twentieth century created a new dandyism of the dispossessed: poor, unemployed and undermployed people who, though they lacked money, had free time with which to pursue their passions. Like the dandies of the nienteenth century, the zoot suiters were alienated from teh mainstream of their society: they were the children and grandchildren of immigrants, stripped of the language and culture of their ancestors and held in wary contempt by their adopted homeland or african american migrants feleeing the unapologetic racism of the rural south for the disdainful racism of the northern cities.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Morehouse College sociologist E. Franklin Frazier wrote in 1995 trhat the \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93black bourgeoisie\u8221\'94 had created a \u8220\'93world of make-believe\u8221\'94 centered around \u8220\'93the activities of those persons who constitute its \u8216\'91society\u8217\'92.\u8221\'94 Black \u8220\'93society\u8221\'94 was defined by exclusive social clubs, dinner and coktail parties, debutate balls, awards ceremonies, and the black fraternity and sorority system of black colleges and universities. Frazier, writing in the psirit and tradition of the Thorstein Veblen\u8217\'92s Theory of the Leisure Class, first published Bourgeoisie Noire for a French-speaking audience, but it was the 1957 English language translasion, Black Bourgeosie, that found a large audience among the community that was the subject of the book. Like Veblen was for the elite of Gilded Age, Frazier was unsparing in his assessment of the Black bourgeoisie. For Frazier, the social rituals of the Black elite were a pathetic imitation fo the grander rituals of white society\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 rituals from which Blacks were, of course, excluded. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85The influence fo Black Bourgeoisie has been long-lived. Just as Marx transformed the term \u8220\'93bourgeoisie\u8221\'94 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  originally the title of propertied urbanites in pre-revolutionary France\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 into the title of an economic class responsible for capitalist exploitation, Frazier turend the term \u8220\'93bourgeoisie\u8221\'94 into a sociological caste defined by its political apathy, social ambition and bland consumerism. Ever since, the term has become something of a free-floating insult and, as a consiequence, politically aware black people have sought to avoid exhibiting any sign of it. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 One of the most controversial of recent school dress codes may be understood in this light. Carlotta Outley Brown, principal of James Madison High School in Houston, Texas, made headlines when she im posed a dress code on teh parents of her students, banning hair rollers, leggings, pajamas, undershirts, and shower capse. Brown observed that adults who arrived on campus inappropriately dressed set a bad example for the children}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Modesty is, by design, always a moving target. ANy group of women, no matter how they are dressed, can and will be divide dinto the virtuous and the sinful, the good girls and the bad girlds, because women carry the guilt of Eve\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s original sin, moralists will always find the bad ones. When high schools enforce overly strict discrimnatory dress codes, they\u8217\'92re doing what schools know how to do best: they are teaching their students. They\u8217\'92re teaching them that yoga pants aren\u8217\'92t just casual attire\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 they\u8217\'92re a sign of sin. They are teaching them by example to idnetify bad girls by what they wear to treat those bad girls badly. }
